Pulled this into a read-only worktree and read the `static/messages.js` notification refactor, the new `notificationclick` handler in `static/sw.js`, and the settings controls in `static/index.html` + `static/panels.js`. The service-worker routing is the right approach for #3196 (iOS PWA can't fire `new Notification()` directly, so `reg.showNotification()` is the only path that works in a standalone PWA). The fallback chain and permission flow are clean. Two concrete things worth addressing.

## 1. `notificationclick` can yank the user off the session they're reading

The handler navigates the **first** window client unconditionally:

```js
self.clients.matchAll({type: 'window', includeUncontrolled: true}).then((clientList) => {
  for (const client of clientList) {
    if ('focus' in client) {
      client.navigate(targetUrl).catch(() => {});
      return client.focus();
    }
  }
  ...
```

If the user has the WebUI open on session A and clicks a background notification for session B, this `client.navigate(targetUrl)` redirects their *current* tab from A to B — losing whatever they were reading/composing in A. Better to prefer a client already on the target URL, and only navigate as a last resort:

```js
const target = clientList.find(c => c.url === targetUrl);
if (target) return target.focus();
const any = clientList.find(c => 'focus' in c);
if (any) { any.navigate(targetUrl).catch(()=>{}); return any.focus(); }
if (self.clients.openWindow) return self.clients.openWindow(targetUrl);
```

Also note `WindowClient.navigate()` only resolves for clients the SW controls and same-origin URLs — the `.catch(()=>{})` swallows the rejection but then `client.focus()` still lands the user on the wrong (un-navigated) session with no error. Worth confirming the focus target actually moved.

## 2. `_notificationOptions` reads the live session, not the completed one

```js
function _notificationOptions(body){
  const sid=S&&S.session&&S.session.session_id;
  const url=sid?`${location.origin}${_sessionUrlForSid(sid)}`:location.href;
```

`sid` is the *currently active* `S.session`, but `sendBrowserNotification` fires when a response completes — which for a background/queued turn may not be the session the user is now viewing. The notification's deep-link (`data.url`) and `tag` (`hermes-${sid}`) would then point at the foreground session rather than the one that actually finished. If the caller has the completing session id available, threading it into `sendBrowserNotification(title, body, {sid})` and using that for the URL/tag would make the deep-link correct. `_sessionUrlForSid` itself is fine — it's the real helper at `static/sessions.js:1807`.

## Smaller notes

- The `{force:true}` test path correctly bypasses the `document.hidden` gate so "Send test" works with the tab focused — good call, and the test asserts it (`test_settings_expose_permission_and_test_controls`).
- `sw.js` changes ride the existing `CACHE_NAME = 'hermes-shell-__WEBUI_VERSION__'` injection (`static/sw.js:10`), so the new handler ships on the next version bump without a manual cache-key change. No action needed, just confirming the SW will actually update.
- CI green across all 9 shards; the regression file is string-assertion style consistent with the repo.

Net: solid PWA slice. The two items above are correctness (right session, don't hijack the active tab) rather than style — I'd want #1 fixed before merge since hijacking the foreground tab is a real regression for multi-session users.

Read the diff in a read-only worktree against `static/ui.js` on `origin/master`. The two-part approach is correct: stash bare `file://` URLs alongside the existing `MEDIA:` tokens in the pre-pass, then decode them to a local path in the restore pass so they fall through to the existing `api/media?path=...` branch (`static/ui.js:3500-3503`). That reuses the authenticated route rather than emitting a browser-dead `file://` anchor. Good.

## Stash regex — scoping looks right, two edge cases
```js
s=s.replace(/(^|\s)(file:\/\/[^\s<>"')\]]+)/g,(_,lead,raw_ref)=>{
  media_stash.push(raw_ref);
  return lead+'\x00D'+(media_stash.length-1)+'\x00';
});
```
The `(^|\s)` anchor + excluding `)` and `]` from the char class is the right call to avoid swallowing `[label](file://...)` markdown anchors — those still hit the link path. Two things to consider:

1. **Trailing punctuation.** The class allows `.`, so `file:///tmp/out.png.` at end of a sentence captures the trailing period into the path, producing `api/media?path=...png.` → 404. The `MEDIA:` token path has the same wart, so not a regression, but a trailing `[.,;:]` trim would be a cheap hardening.
2. **Literal spaces.** A well-formed `file://` URL percent-encodes spaces, and the decode handles that via `decodeURIComponent`. But a tool emitting a raw `file:///tmp/My File.png` (literal space) gets truncated at the space. Probably acceptable — just noting it.

## Decode fallback chain is sound
```js
const u=new URL(ref);
ref=decodeURIComponent(u.pathname||ref.replace(/^file:\/\//i,''));
```
`new URL('file:///tmp/x.png').pathname` → `/tmp/x.png`, and the nested try/catch degrades gracefully if `URL` or `decodeURIComponent` throws. One note: for a `file://host/path` form (non-empty authority), `u.pathname` drops the host — fine for the local-artifact case this targets (`file:///`), just be aware UNC-style refs won't round-trip.

## Security note (not introduced here)
This widens what reaches `api/media` to include bare `file://` URLs from assistant/tool output. The `/api/media` handler (`api/routes.py:8001`) is auth-gated and confines to an allowlist (`HERMES_HOME`, `/tmp`, workspace — `:8044`), so no traversal outside that. But as #3234 documents, the whole `HERMES_HOME` being allowlisted means state files like `webui/settings.json` are reachable today. This PR doesn't change `_handle_media` and so doesn't *introduce* that, but it does broaden reachability — worth landing the #3234 confinement fix alongside or shortly after this.

Test coverage asserts the regex and decode substrings are present in `static/ui.js`; that guards against accidental removal. LGTM modulo the trailing-punctuation nit.

Pulled the branch into a read-only worktree and read both hunks against `origin/master`.

## The `messages.js` fix is correct

On master, the `done` handler only sets `_terminalStateReached=true` up front and defers `_streamFinalized=true` into `_finishDone` (`static/messages.js:1831`), which runs *after* the fade delay:

```js
source.addEventListener('done',e=>{
  if(_streamFinalized) return;
  _terminalStateReached=true;       // master stops here before the fade
  ...
  const _finishDone=()=>{ _streamFinalized=true; ... };
  if(_shouldUseStreamFade()&&assistantBody){ _drainStreamFadeBeforeDone(_finishDone); return; }
  _finishDone();
});
```

During the fade window a `stream_end` arrives, sees `_streamFinalized===false`, and falls through to `_restoreSettledSession()` (`messages.js:2017`), which refetches `/api/session` and overwrites `S.messages` with whatever the server has persisted so far — the loss in #3195. Hoisting `_streamFinalized=true` to the top of `done` makes the `stream_end` guard catch it (`messages.js:2003`):

```js
source.addEventListener('stream_end',async e=>{
  if(_streamFinalized){ _closeSource(source); return; }
```

I checked the fade isn't broken by the early flag: `_drainStreamFadeBeforeDone`'s `step()` loop (`messages.js:1421-1450`) drives the animation through `_renderStreamingFadeMarkdown` directly and does **not** gate on `_streamFinalized`. The flag only short-circuits the `_scheduleRender` rAF path (`messages.js:1501/1516/1526`), which is exactly what you want suppressed once `done` has the canonical session. There's also the `#2852` post-await re-check inside `_restoreSettledSession` (`messages.js:2349`) as a second line of defense. So the ordering change is sound.

## The `ui.js` hunk is a dead no-op — please drop it

```js
      return;
    }
+  } else {
+  }
```

This adds an empty `else {}` to the session-HTML-cache branch in `renderMessages` (`static/ui.js:6271`). It has no effect and looks like a leftover from debugging. It should be removed so the diff is just the one-line `messages.js` change.

## Suggestion

Worth a one-line CHANGELOG `[Unreleased]` entry referencing #3195, and if feasible a regression test asserting that a `stream_end` delivered during the fade window does not call `_restoreSettledSession` once `done` has fired. Net: the core fix is right and minimal — just strip the `ui.js` artifact.

Reviewed read-only against `origin/master` (`79145c0a`). The fix is correct and minimal — I traced the full function on both master and the PR head plus the single caller, and the new logic resolves the duplicate-row race cleanly.

## What the old code did

On master, `_markSessionCompletedInList` matched *either* id with a single `findIndex`:

```js
// static/sessions.js:364 (master)
const idx = _allSessions.findIndex(s => s && (s.session_id === finalSid || s.session_id === previousSid));
```

When both the preserved-snapshot row (`old`) and the continuation row (`new`) are present during compression completion, `findIndex` returns whichever sits earlier in `_allSessions`. If `old` precedes `new`, `idx` lands on `old`, the function rewrites that row's `session_id` to `finalSid`, and the *already-present* `new` row is left untouched — two rows now share `session_id === 'new'`. That's the duplicate sidebar entry.

## What the PR fixes

The two changes line up exactly with the failure:

```js
// prefer the continuation/tip row, fall back to old only if tip absent
const finalIdx = _allSessions.findIndex(s => s && s.session_id === finalSid);
const previousIdx = previousSid ? _allSessions.findIndex(s => s && s.session_id === previousSid) : -1;
const idx = finalIdx >= 0 ? finalIdx : previousIdx;
```

and the post-update sweep that drops any remaining `previousSid` rows other than the one just written:

```js
for (let i = _allSessions.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  if (i !== idx && _allSessions[i] && _allSessions[i].session_id === previousSid) {
    _allSessions.splice(i, 1);
  }
}
```

Two details I checked that make this safe:

- The backwards iteration is the right call — splicing from the tail forward would shift indices under the loop. Going high→low keeps the remaining indices valid. And since `idx` isn't referenced again after this block (the rest of the function keys off `finalSid`), an index shift below `idx` is harmless even though `i !== idx` already protects the updated row.
- In the fallback branch (`finalIdx < 0`, only `old` present), the row at `idx` is rewritten to `finalSid` *before* the sweep, so `_allSessions[idx].session_id === previousSid` is no longer true and the guard never needs to fire — correct by construction.

The sole caller passes `previousSid` as the pre-completion `activeSid` (`static/messages.js:1844`), which is the compression-rotation case this targets, so the new behavior can't regress the non-compression path (where `previousSid === finalSid` and the `if (previousSid && previousSid !== finalSid)` block is skipped entirely).

## Test

`tests/test_sidebar_completion_dedupe.py` extracts the function via brace-matching and runs it under Node with both rows seeded — asserting the final list collapses to `['new']` with the merged title/count and `oldStreaming === false`. That's a faithful regression for the exact duplicate case. The companion edit to `test_issue856_...::test_sidebar_cache_completion_handles_compression_session_rotation` swaps the old `s.session_id === finalSid || s.session_id === previousSid` source-invariant assertion for the three new lines, so the guard stays pinned. Looks ready to merge.

## Summary

Read this PR's diff against `origin/master` (the two added blocks in `static/sessions.js` — one in `_loadOlderMessages`, one in `_ensureAllMessagesLoaded`). The Claude write-up nails the root cause and I confirmed it on master: the fallback that derives tool cards from messages only runs when `S.toolCalls` is empty (`static/ui.js:6672`, `if(!S.busy && (!S.toolCalls||!S.toolCalls.length))`), and it stamps each card with `assistant_msg_idx: rawIdx` — the index into the *current* `S.messages`. When `_loadOlderMessages` prepends older pages, those `rawIdx` values now point at the wrong (older) rows, and because `S.toolCalls` is non-empty the fallback never re-derives. So the modern-Claude-session path (per-message `tool_use`, server sends `tool_calls=[]`) is exactly the case your reporter hits, and clearing `S.toolCalls` so the fallback rebuilds with fresh indices is correct.

## Code reference

Your scroll-path fix:

```js
const _loadedTc = (responseSession.tool_calls || []).filter(Boolean);
if (_loadedTc.length > 0) {
  S.toolCalls = _loadedTc.map(tc => ({...tc, done: true}));
} else {
  S.toolCalls = [];
}
```

The `else` branch (clear → fallback rebuilds) is the half that fixes #3120 for modern sessions, and it's solid.

## Diagnosis — the legacy branch has a coordinate mismatch

The `if (_loadedTc.length > 0)` branch adopts the server's `tool_calls` verbatim. But on master, the server ships **absolute** `assistant_msg_idx` for windowed loads. In `api/routes.py` `_tool_calls_for_message_window` filters to the window but does *not* rebase:

```python
if start_idx <= assistant_idx < end_idx:
    filtered.append(tool_call)   # assistant_msg_idx still absolute
```

Meanwhile the consumer at `static/ui.js:6415` matches `tc.assistant_msg_idx` against `renderedRawIdxs`, which are 0-based into `S.messages`. So for a legacy (pre-`tool_use`) session loaded through a paged window where `_messages_offset > 0`, the adopted cards anchor off by `+offset` and won't render on the right row. `test_session_tail_payload.py::test_msg_before_window_keeps_only_that_page_session_tool_calls` documents this: it asserts the payload keeps `assistant_msg_idx=1` with `_messages_offset=1` — i.e. absolute coordinates.

In `_loadOlderMessages` specifically, after a successful suffix-expand `nextMessages` starts at the transcript head only when fully expanded; in the partial-window/`msg_before` fallback path (sessions.js ~1483-1492) `nextMessages = [...olderMsgs, ...S.messages]` with `_oldestIdx` reset from `_messages_offset`, so absolute indices don't line up with the prepended array's 0-base.

## Recommendation

Either rebase on adopt:

```js
const off = responseSession._messages_offset || 0;
S.toolCalls = _loadedTc.map(tc => {
  const c = {...tc, done: true};
  if (Number.isInteger(c.assistant_msg_idx)) c.assistant_msg_idx -= off;
  return c;
});
```

or fix the backend to emit window-relative indices (that's the route the sibling PR #3187 takes). Don't do both — #3187 currently rebases on *both* sides and double-subtracts the offset; I left details there. The cleanest convergence is one coordinate authority: backend stays absolute + frontend rebases (your PR + the snippet above), **or** backend rebases + frontend adopts verbatim.

For `_ensureAllMessagesLoaded` your comment is right that `rawIdx = absolute index` when everything is loaded, so the verbatim adopt is correct there — `_messages_offset` is 0 on a full load.

## Test plan

The modern-session path (the actual #3120 repro) is well covered by the `else` branch. To lock the legacy branch, add a paged-load case: legacy session, two assistant turns with session-level `tool_calls` at absolute idx 1 and 3, load with `msg_before`/`msg_limit` so `_messages_offset=1`, and assert both cards land on their correct rendered rows rather than shifting up by the offset. A JSDOM render assertion is ideal since the bug is purely in index→row mapping.

## Summary

Read the full diff at this PR's HEAD against `origin/master` — the new `_session_counts_toward_pin_quota()` in `api/routes.py`, both quota call sites in `handle_post`, the `_preserve_pre_compression_snapshot` changes in `api/streaming.py`, and the test updates. The two-sided approach matches the root cause in #3181 exactly, and I traced both halves against master. This looks correct and mergeable. A few confirmations and one non-blocking note below.

## Code reference

The quota helper is the important piece, and it's the right backstop:

```python
def _session_counts_toward_pin_quota(session) -> bool:
    if not _session_field(session, "pinned", False):
        return False
    if _session_field(session, "archived", False):
        return False
    if isinstance(session, dict):
        row = session
    elif hasattr(session, "compact"):
        row = session.compact()
    else:
        row = {...}
    return not _hide_from_default_sidebar(row)
```

I verified `_hide_from_default_sidebar` at `api/models.py:2352-2360`:

```python
if bool(session.get('pre_compression_snapshot')):
    return not bool(session.get('_show_pre_compression_snapshot'))
```

and confirmed `Session.compact()` (`api/models.py:844`, field at ~881) emits `pre_compression_snapshot`, so the live-object → `compact()` → `_hide_from_default_sidebar` path correctly excludes hidden snapshots.

## Why the backstop matters (worth calling out)

The `api/streaming.py` change (`s.pinned = False` on both the rename branch and the `Session.load` mark branch) only prevents *new* corruption. The reporter in #3181 already has on-disk snapshots carrying stale `pinned: true` from before this fix. The quota helper handles those: at `/api/session/pin` time it re-derives visibility from `_hide_from_default_sidebar` regardless of the persisted `pinned` flag, so existing corrupted snapshots stop consuming quota immediately on upgrade — no migration needed. Good design; I'd note it in the changelog/PR so reviewers know existing state self-heals.

## Non-blocking note — compact() omits source_tag

`Session.compact()` does not emit `source_tag` (I grepped the dict body — only `pre_compression_snapshot` is present, not `source`/`source_tag`). For the snapshot case this is fine. But the helper's `_hide_from_default_sidebar` also hides cron sessions via `source == 'cron' or sid.startswith('cron_')`. For a *live* pinned cron `Session` reaching this through the `compact()` branch, only the `sid.startswith('cron_')` test can fire — the `source_tag == 'cron'` test sees nothing. In practice cron session ids are prefixed `cron_`, so it still works; just flagging that the dict-input branch (which explicitly passes `source_tag`) and the compact branch aren't perfectly symmetric. Out of scope for #3181, but if you ever pin-cap cron rows it'd matter.

## Test plan

The added coverage is appropriate: dict input, `SimpleNamespace` (in-memory) input, and the persist/restore round-trip in `test_compression_snapshot_runtime_clear.py` asserting `saved["pinned"] is False` while the live session's `pinned` is restored to `True`. One gap worth closing: there's no route-level test that actually drives `/api/session/pin` with a hidden pinned snapshot present in `SESSIONS` and asserts a visible session can still be pinned up to the cap. The unit tests cover the helper in isolation, but an end-to-end pin-cap test (extending `test_session_pin_endpoint_caps_pinned_sessions_at_three`) would lock the integration where `persisted_pinned_ids` and the `SESSIONS` merge meet.

Reading `list_workspace_suggestions()` at `api/workspace.py:442` on `origin/master` plus the diff, this is a clean display-only fix for #3173 and the trust boundary stays intact.

## The gap on master

Master normalizes a `~/` prefix to an absolute path early:

```python
if raw.startswith("~"):
    target = Path(raw).expanduser()
...
def add(path: Path) -> None:
    value = str(path)   # <-- always absolute
```

so every suggestion comes back as `/home/<user>/...` even when the user typed `~/`, replacing their tilde input with a long absolute path. The fix keeps scanning rooted in absolute paths (so the `_trusted_workspace_roots()` / `_is_within(parent_resolved, anchor_root)` checks below are unchanged) and only reformats the *display* value:

```python
def format_suggestion(path: Path) -> str:
    if preserve_tilde and home_root is not None:
        try:
            rel = path.resolve().relative_to(home_root)
            if str(rel) == ".":
                return "~"
            return "~/" + rel.as_posix()
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
    return str(path)
```

## Why the trust boundary is preserved

The reformatting only triggers when `raw.startswith("~")` and the resolved path is `relative_to(home_root)`. A directory outside home raises `ValueError` and falls back to `str(path)`, so you can't smuggle a non-home path into a `~/`-prefixed display. And because the absolute `target`/`anchor_root` scan logic is untouched, the existing guards — `_is_within(parent_resolved, anchor_root)` and the `_is_blocked_workspace_path` filtering inside `_trusted_workspace_roots()` — still gate which directories are enumerated. The PR correctly notes saved paths still resolve through server-side validation, so this is purely a suggestion-display change.

## `.resolve()` cost

One minor thing: `format_suggestion` calls `path.resolve()` per suggestion, and the scan already resolved `parent_resolved` once. For the home-root suggestions this is a second resolve per child. With `limit=12` it's negligible, just noting it in case the suggestion cap ever grows — you could pass the already-resolved child through to avoid the re-stat.

## Tests

`test_workspace_suggest_preserves_tilde_prefix` (asserts `"~/Projects" in suggestions`), `..._for_partial_child` (`== ["~/Projects"]`), and `..._keeps_absolute_prefix_absolute` cover the three branches: bare `~/`, `~/partial`, and absolute-prefix-stays-absolute. Assertions match the implemented behavior, and CI is green on 3.11/3.12/3.13. LGTM.

## Summary

Read the full diff at this PR's HEAD against `origin/master` — the `session://` handling in `_smdLinkHref` (`static/messages.js`, streaming path), `_markdownHref`/`_markdownAnchor`/`_isSafeUrl` (`static/ui.js`, settled path), the `_sessionUrlForSid` rewrite target (`static/sessions.js:1791`), and the delegated click handler (`static/ui.js:631-638`). Since this renders links from agent-streamed/untrusted markdown, I focused on the sanitization surface. The scheme handling is sound — confirming below — plus one minor observation on regex breadth.

## Why the scheme handling is safe

The `session://` scheme never reaches the DOM as an attribute value. Both render paths rewrite it to a relative href before sanitization, and `_sessionUrlForSid` (`static/sessions.js:1791-1804`) only ever returns a same-origin path:

```js
function _sessionUrlForSid(sid){
  const encoded=encodeURIComponent(sid);
  let base;
  try{base=new URL(`session/${encoded}`, document.baseURI||window.location.origin+'/');}
  catch(_e){base=new URL(`/session/${encoded}`, window.location.origin);}
  ...
  return base.pathname+base.search+base.hash;
}
```

`base.pathname+base.search+base.hash` strips any scheme/host, so a crafted `session://javascript:...` or `session://evil.com` collapses to a literal `/session/<encoded>` path — no `javascript:` execution, no open redirect. The `_isSafeUrl` allowlist (`static/ui.js:3319-3322`) then only passes relative `session/` forms, never the raw scheme, and the click handler at `static/ui.js:631-638` calls `loadSession(decodeURIComponent(m[1]))`, a local session lookup — it can't navigate off-origin. The id encoding helper also correctly escapes `(`/`)` (`_sessionMarkdownUrlSid`) which `encodeURIComponent` leaves literal and which would otherwise terminate a Markdown link destination early. Good catch in the PR notes.

## Observation — `_isSafeUrl` regex is broader than the feature needs

The settled-path allowlist adds three alternations:

```js
if(!img && (/^api\//i.test(raw) || /^#/.test(raw)
   || /^session\//i.test(raw)
   || /^\/[^?#]*\/session\/[^?#]+/i.test(raw)
   || /^\/session\/[^?#]+/i.test(raw))) return true;
```

The middle pattern `^\/[^?#]*\/session\/[^?#]+` matches *any* same-origin absolute path that merely contains a `/session/` segment (e.g. `/anything/foo/session/x`). It's same-origin so not a security hole, but it's looser than the `_sessionUrlForSid` output you actually emit (which is always rooted at the base path + `session/`). Tightening it to anchor on the known base — or reusing a single shared `_isInternalSessionHref()` predicate for both the allowlist and the `_markdownAnchor` `internal` flag — would prevent drift between the "is this safe" check and the "is this a session link" check. Right now `_markdownAnchor` (`static/ui.js:3309-3313`) computes `internal` with a *third* slightly different regex, so there are three near-duplicate patterns describing the same concept across the file. Consolidating reduces the chance a future edit makes the click handler and the sanitizer disagree.

## Test plan note

`tests/test_session_copy_link_action.py` covers the Markdown-safe id encoding (the `(`/`)` and bracket escaping) well. The gap is the *render-and-sanitize* direction: there's no assertion that a hostile `[x](session://javascript:alert(1))` or `[x](session://../../etc)` input collapses to a safe relative `/session/...` href and that the resulting anchor carries `class="session-link"` without `target=_blank`. A JSDOM test feeding those through `renderMd()` and asserting the final `href` + class would lock the sanitizer contract that the manual analysis above relies on, since the current Python tests can't exercise the JS rewrite path.

Net: the security model holds; the suggestions are hardening/maintainability, not blockers.

@nesquena-hermes coming from a front-end background I completely understand the concern. I've added screenshots at different viewports to the description and tested on my smartphone.

In my opinion (as a heavy user of the profile feature) it makes sense that on small viewports the profile switcher still comes before the hamburger menu — the profile is first in the workflow logic (you choose which agent you are before you navigate), even if it can seem counterintuitive to someone unfamiliar with the profile concept who expects the burger menu first on the left. It also keeps the same Grid layout model across viewports — just the column sizing adapts. That said, placing the hamburger menu first on the left would also work and wouldn’t be a big issue. The important point is to fill the gap in the UI/UX. Feel free to think about it thoughtfully first :)

On the UX level I personally experience it as a great improvement in my daily use.

Reading `static/sessions.js:119-126` on `origin/master` plus `getModelLabel()` at `static/ui.js:2819` and `_formatGatewayModelLabel()` at `:2867`, the one-line fix is correct and the reasoning in the PR holds up.

## The fallback path

On master, the no-routing fallback returns the raw `s.model`:

```js
function _formatSessionModelWithGateway(s){
  if(!s||!s.model)return'';
  const routing=(typeof _latestGatewayRoutingForSession==='function')?_latestGatewayRoutingForSession(s):(s.gateway_routing||null);
  if(typeof _formatGatewayModelLabel==='function'){
    return _formatGatewayModelLabel(s.model,s.model,routing)||s.model;   // <-- raw fallback
  }
  return s.model;
}
```

`_formatGatewayModelLabel` short-circuits to `''` when `routing` is falsy (`ui.js:2868`: `if(!routing)return'';`), so on any session without gateway routing the `|| s.model` branch fires and the list shows the unprocessed ID — including a `@provider:` prefix.

## Why `getModelLabel()` is the right fallback

`getModelLabel()` already strips that prefix (`ui.js:2845`):

```js
let _last = modelId.split('/').pop() || modelId;
// Strip @provider: prefix if present (e.g. @ollama-cloud:kimi-k2.6)
if (_last.startsWith('@') && _last.includes(':')) _last = _last.split(':').slice(1).join(':');
```

